<p align=3D"CENTER">
<b>BERKSHIRE HATHAWAY INC.</b>
</p>

<pre>

<b>To the Shareholders of Berkshire Hathaway Inc.:</b>


     Our per-share book value increased 20.3% during 1992.  Over=20
the last 28 years (that is, since present management took over)=20
book value has grown from $19 to $7,745, or at a rate of 23.6%=20
compounded annually.

     During the year, Berkshire's net worth increased by $1.52=20
billion.  More than 98% of this gain came from earnings and=20
appreciation of portfolio securities, with the remainder coming=20
from the issuance of new stock.  These shares were issued as a=20
result of our calling our convertible debentures for redemption=20
on January 4, 1993, and of some holders electing to receive=20
common shares rather than the cash that was their alternative. =20
Most holders of the debentures who converted into common waited=20
until January to do it, but a few made the move in December and=20
therefore received shares in 1992.  To sum up what happened to=20
the $476 million of bonds we had outstanding:  $25 million were=20
converted into shares before yearend; $46 million were converted=20
in January; and $405 million were redeemed for cash.  The=20
conversions were made at $11,719 per share, so altogether we=20
issued 6,106 shares.

     Berkshire now has 1,152,547 shares outstanding.  That=20
compares, you will be interested to know, to 1,137,778 shares=20
outstanding on October 1, 1964, the beginning of the fiscal year=20
during which Buffett Partnership, Ltd. acquired control of the=20
company.

     We have a firm policy about issuing shares of Berkshire,=20
doing so only when we receive as much value as we give.  Equal=20
value, however, has not been easy to obtain, since we have always=20
valued our shares highly.  So be it:  We wish to increase=20
Berkshire's size only when doing that also increases the wealth=20
of its owners.

    Those two objectives do not necessarily go hand-in-hand as an=20
amusing but value-destroying experience in our past illustrates.=20
On that occasion, we had a significant investment in a bank=20
whose management was hell-bent on expansion.  (Aren't they all?)=20
When our bank wooed a smaller bank, its owner demanded a stock=20
swap on a basis that valued the acquiree's net worth and earning=20
power at over twice that of the acquirer's.  Our management -=20
visibly in heat - quickly capitulated.  The owner of the acquiree=20
then insisted on one other condition:  "You must promise me," he=20
said in effect, "that once our merger is done and I have become a=20
major shareholder, you'll never again make a deal this dumb."

     You will remember that our goal is to increase our per-share=20
intrinsic value - for which our book value is a conservative, but=20
useful, proxy - at a 15% annual rate.  This objective, however,=20
cannot be attained in a smooth manner.  Smoothness is=20
particularly elusive because of the accounting rules that apply=20
to the common stocks owned by our insurance companies, whose=20
portfolios represent a high proportion of Berkshire's net worth.=20
Since 1979, generally accepted accounting principles (GAAP) have=20
required that these securities be valued at their market prices=20
(less an adjustment for tax on any net unrealized appreciation)=20
rather than at the lower of cost or market.  Run-of-the-mill=20
fluctuations in equity prices therefore cause our annual results=20
to gyrate, especially in comparison to those of the typical=20
industrial company.

     To illustrate just how volatile our progress has been - and=20
to indicate the impact that market movements have on short-term=20
results - we show on the facing page our annual change in per-
share net worth and compare it with the annual results (including=20
dividends) of the S&amp;P 500.

     You should keep at least three points in mind as you=20
evaluate this data.  The first point concerns the many businesses=20
we operate whose annual earnings are unaffected by changes in=20
stock market valuations.  The impact of these businesses on both=20
our absolute and relative performance has changed over the years.=20
Early on, returns from our textile operation, which then=20
represented a significant portion of our net worth, were a major=20
drag on performance, averaging far less than would have been the=20
case if the money invested in that business had instead been=20
invested in the S&amp;P 500.  In more recent years, as we assembled=20
our collection of exceptional businesses run by equally=20
exceptional managers, the returns from our operating businesses=20
have been high - usually well in excess of the returns achieved=20
by the S&amp;P.

     A second important factor to consider - and one that=20
significantly hurts our relative performance - is that both the=20
income and capital gains from our securities are burdened by a=20
substantial corporate tax liability whereas the S&amp;P returns are=20
pre-tax.  To comprehend the damage, imagine that Berkshire had=20
owned nothing other than the S&amp;P index during the 28-year period=20
covered. In that case, the tax bite would have caused our=20
corporate performance to be appreciably below the record shown in=20
the table for the S&amp;P.  Under present tax laws, a gain for the=20
S&amp;P of 18% delivers a corporate holder of that index a return=20
well short of 13%.  And this problem would be intensified if=20
corporate tax rates were to rise.  This is a structural=20
disadvantage we simply have to live with; there is no antidote=20
for it.

     The third point incorporates two predictions:  Charlie=20
Munger, Berkshire's Vice Chairman and my partner, and I are=20
virtually certain that the return over the next decade from an=20
investment in the S&amp;P index will be far less than that of the=20
past decade, and we are dead certain that the drag exerted by=20
Berkshire's expanding capital base will substantially reduce our=20
historical advantage relative to the index.

     Making the first prediction goes somewhat against our grain:=20
We've long felt that the only value of stock forecasters is to=20
make fortune tellers look good.  Even now, Charlie and I continue=20
to believe that short-term market forecasts are poison and should=20
be kept locked up in a safe place, away from children and also=20
from grown-ups who behave in the market like children.  However,=20
it is clear that stocks cannot forever overperform their=20
underlying businesses, as they have so dramatically done for some=20
time, and that fact makes us quite confident of our forecast that=20
the rewards from investing in stocks over the next decade will be=20
significantly smaller than they were in the last.  Our second=20
conclusion - that an increased capital base will act as an anchor=20
on our relative performance - seems incontestable.  The only open=20
question is whether we can drag the anchor along at some=20
tolerable, though slowed, pace.

     We will continue to experience considerable volatility in=20
our annual results.  That's assured by the general volatility of=20
the stock market, by the concentration of our equity holdings in=20
just a few companies, and by certain business decisions we have=20
made, most especially our move to commit large resources to=20
super-catastrophe insurance.  We not only accept this volatility=20
but welcome it:  A tolerance for short-term swings improves our=20
long-term prospects.  In baseball lingo, our performance=20
yardstick is slugging percentage, not batting average.

<b>The Salomon Interlude</b>

     Last June, I stepped down as Interim Chairman of Salomon Inc=20
after ten months in the job.  You can tell from Berkshire's 1991-
92 results that the company didn't miss me while I was gone.  But=20
the reverse isn't true:  I missed Berkshire and am delighted to=20
be back full-time.  There is no job in the world that is more fun=20
than running Berkshire and I count myself lucky to be where I am.

     The Salomon post, though far from fun, was interesting and=20
worthwhile:  In Fortune's annual survey of America's Most Admired=20
Corporations, conducted last September, Salomon ranked second=20
among 311 companies in the degree to which it improved its=20
reputation.  Additionally, Salomon Brothers, the securities=20
subsidiary of Salomon Inc, reported record pre-tax earnings last=20
year - 34% above the previous high.

     Many people helped in the resolution of Salomon's problems=20
and the righting of the firm, but a few clearly deserve special=20
mention.  It is no exaggeration to say that without the combined=20
efforts of Salomon executives Deryck Maughan, Bob Denham, Don=20
Howard, and John Macfarlane, the firm very probably would not=20
have survived.  In their work, these men were tireless,=20
effective, supportive and selfless, and I will forever be=20
grateful to them.

     Salomon's lead lawyer in its Government matters, Ron Olson=20
of Munger, Tolles &amp; Olson, was also key to our success in getting=20
through this trouble.  The firm's problems were not only severe,=20
but complex.  At least five authorities - the SEC, the Federal=20
Reserve Bank of New York, the U.S. Treasury, the U.S. Attorney=20
for the Southern District of New York, and the Antitrust Division=20
of the Department of Justice - had important concerns about=20
Salomon.  If we were to resolve our problems in a coordinated and=20
prompt manner, we needed a lawyer with exceptional legal,=20
business and human skills.  Ron had them all.

<b>Acquisitions</b>

     Of all our activities at Berkshire, the most exhilarating=20
for Charlie and me is the acquisition of a business with=20
excellent economic characteristics and a management that we like,=20
trust and admire.  Such acquisitions are not easy to make but we=20
look for them constantly.  In the search, we adopt the same=20
attitude one might find appropriate in looking for a spouse:  It=20
pays to be active, interested and open-minded, but it does not=20
pay to be in a hurry.

     In the past, I've observed that many acquisition-hungry=20
managers were apparently mesmerized by their childhood reading of=20
the story about the frog-kissing princess.  Remembering her=20
success, they pay dearly for the right to kiss corporate toads,=20
expecting wondrous transfigurations.  Initially, disappointing=20
results only deepen their desire to round up new toads. =20
("Fanaticism," said Santyana, "consists of redoubling your effort=20
when you've forgotten your aim.")  Ultimately, even the most=20
optimistic manager must face reality.  Standing knee-deep in=20
unresponsive toads, he then announces an enormous "restructuring"=20
charge.  In this corporate equivalent of a Head Start program,=20
the CEO receives the education but the stockholders pay the=20
tuition.

     In my early days as a manager I, too, dated a few toads. =20
They were cheap dates - I've never been much of a sport - but my=20
results matched those of acquirers who courted higher-priced=20
toads.  I kissed and they croaked.

     After several failures of this type, I finally remembered=20
some useful advice I once got from a golf pro (who, like all pros=20
who have had anything to do with my game, wishes to remain=20
anonymous).  Said the pro:  "Practice doesn't make perfect;=20
practice makes permanent."  And thereafter I revised my strategy=20
and tried to buy good businesses at fair prices rather than fair=20
businesses at good prices.

     Last year, in December, we made an acquisition that is a=20
prototype of what we now look for.  The purchase was 82% of=20
Central States Indemnity, an insurer that makes monthly payments=20
for credit-card holders who are unable themselves to pay because=20
they have become disabled or unemployed.  Currently the company's=20
annual premiums are about $90 million and profits about $10=20
million.  Central States is based in Omaha and managed by Bill=20
Kizer, a friend of mine for over 35 years.  The Kizer family -=20
which includes sons Bill, Dick and John - retains 18% ownership=20
of the business and will continue to run things just as it has in=20
the past.  We could not be associated with better people.

     Coincidentally, this latest acquisition has much in common=20
with our first, made 26 years ago.  At that time, we purchased=20
another Omaha insurer, National Indemnity Company (along with a=20
small sister company) from Jack Ringwalt, another long-time=20
friend.  Jack had built the business from scratch and, as was the=20
case with Bill Kizer, thought of me when he wished to sell. =20
(Jack's comment at the time:  "If I don't sell the company, my=20
executor will, and I'd rather pick the home for it.")  National=20
Indemnity was an outstanding business when we bought it and=20
continued to be under Jack's management.  Hollywood has had good=20
luck with sequels; I believe we, too, will.

     Berkshire's acquisition criteria are described on page 23. =20
Beyond purchases made by the parent company, however, our=20
subsidiaries sometimes make small "add-on" acquisitions that=20
extend their product lines or distribution capabilities.  In this=20
manner, we enlarge the domain of managers we already know to be=20
outstanding - and that's a low-risk and high-return proposition.=20
We made five acquisitions of this type in 1992, and one was not=20
so small:  At yearend, H. H. Brown purchased Lowell Shoe Company,=20
a business with $90 million in sales that makes Nursemates, a=20
leading line of shoes for nurses, and other kinds of shoes as=20
well.  Our operating managers will continue to look for add-on=20
opportunities, and we would expect these to contribute modestly=20
to Berkshire's value in the future.

     Then again, a trend has emerged that may make further=20
acquisitions difficult.  The parent company made one purchase in=20
1991, buying H. H. Brown, which is run by Frank Rooney, who has=20
eight children.  In 1992 our only deal was with Bill Kizer,=20
father of nine.  It won't be easy to keep this string going in=20
1993.

