     A large amount of additional information about these=20
businesses is given on pages 37-47, where you will also find our=20
segment earnings reported on a GAAP basis.  Our goal is to give you=20
all of the financial information that Charlie and I consider=20
significant in making our own evaluation of Berkshire.

<b>"Look-Through" Earnings</b>

     We've previously discussed look-through earnings, which=20
consist of: (1) the operating earnings reported in the previous=20
section, plus; (2) the retained operating earnings of major=20
investees that, under GAAP accounting, are not reflected in our=20
profits, less; (3) an allowance for the tax that would be paid by=20
Berkshire if these retained earnings of investees had instead been=20
distributed to us.  Though no single figure can be perfect, we=20
believe that the look-through number more accurately portrays the=20
earnings of Berkshire than does the GAAP number.

     I've told you that over time look-through earnings must=20
increase at about 15% annually if our intrinsic business value is=20
to grow at that rate.  Our look-through earnings in 1992 were $604=20
million, and they will need to grow to more than $1.8 billion by=20
the year 2000 if we are to meet that 15% goal.  For us to get=20
there, our operating subsidiaries and investees must deliver=20
excellent performances, and we must exercise some skill in capital=20
allocation as well.

     We cannot promise to achieve the $1.8 billion target.  Indeed,=20
we may not even come close to it.  But it does guide our decision-
making:  When we allocate capital today, we are thinking about what=20
will maximize look-through earnings in 2000.

     We do not, however, see this long-term focus as eliminating=20
the need for us to achieve decent short-term results as well. =20
After all, we were thinking long-range thoughts five or ten years=20
ago, and the moves we made then should now be paying off.  If=20
plantings made confidently are repeatedly followed by disappointing=20
harvests, something is wrong with the farmer.  (Or perhaps with the=20
farm:  Investors should understand that for certain companies, and=20
even for some industries, there simply is <i>no</i> good long-term=20
strategy.)  Just as you should be suspicious of managers who pump=20
up short-term earnings by accounting maneuvers, asset sales and the=20
like, so also should you be suspicious of those managers who fail=20
to deliver for extended periods and blame it on their long-term=20
focus.  (Even Alice, after listening to the Queen lecture her about=20
"jam tomorrow," finally insisted, "It must come sometimes to jam=20
today.")

