     The combined ratio represents total insurance costs (losses=20
incurred plus expenses) compared to revenue from premiums:  A=20
ratio below 100 indicates an underwriting profit, and one above=20
100 indicates a loss.  The higher the ratio, the worse the year.=20
When the investment income that an insurer earns from holding=20
policyholders' funds ("the float") is taken into account, a=20
combined ratio in the 106 - 110 range typically produces an=20
overall break-even result, exclusive of earnings on the funds=20
provided by shareholders.

     About four points in the industry's 1992 combined ratio can=20
be attributed to Hurricane Andrew, which caused the largest=20
insured loss in history.  Andrew destroyed a few small insurers.=20
Beyond that, it awakened some larger companies to the fact that=20
their reinsurance protection against catastrophes was far from=20
adequate.  (It's only when the tide goes out that you learn who's=20
been swimming naked.)  One major insurer escaped insolvency=20
solely because it had a wealthy parent that could promptly supply=20
a massive transfusion of capital.

     Bad as it was, however, Andrew could easily have been far=20
more damaging if it had hit Florida 20 or 30 miles north of where=20
it actually did and had hit Louisiana further east than was the=20
case.  All in all, many companies will rethink their reinsurance=20
programs in light of the Andrew experience.

     As you know we are a large writer - perhaps the largest in=20
the world - of "super-cat" coverages, which are the policies that=20
other insurance companies buy to protect themselves against major=20
catastrophic losses.  Consequently, we too took our lumps from=20
Andrew, suffering losses from it of about $125 million, an amount=20
roughly equal to our 1992 super-cat premium income.  Our other=20
super-cat losses, though, were negligible.  This line of business=20
therefore produced an overall loss of only $2 million for the=20
year.  (In addition, our investee, GEICO, suffered a net loss=20
from Andrew, after reinsurance recoveries and tax savings, of=20
about $50 million, of which our share is roughly $25 million. =20
This loss did not affect our operating earnings, but did reduce=20
our look-through earnings.)

     In last year's report I told you that I hoped that our=20
super-cat business would over time achieve a 10% profit margin. =20
But I also warned you that in any given year the line was likely=20
to be "either enormously profitable or enormously unprofitable."=20
Instead, both 1991 and 1992 have come in close to a break-even=20
level.  Nonetheless, I see these results as aberrations and stick=20
with my prediction of huge annual swings in profitability from=20
this business.

     Let me remind you of some characteristics of our super-cat=20
policies.  Generally, they are activated only when two things=20
happen.  First, the direct insurer or reinsurer we protect must=20
suffer losses of a given amount - that's the policyholder's=20
"retention" - from a catastrophe; and second, industry-wide=20
insured losses from the catastrophe must exceed some minimum=20
level, which usually is $3 billion or more.  In most cases, the=20
policies we issue cover only a specific geographical area, such=20
as a portion of the U.S., the entire U.S., or everywhere other=20
than the U.S.  Also, many policies are not activated by the first=20
super-cat that meets the policy terms, but instead cover only a=20
"second-event" or even a third- or fourth-event.  Finally, some=20
policies are triggered only by a catastrophe of a specific type,=20
such as an earthquake.  Our exposures are large: We have one=20
policy that calls for us to pay $100 million to the policyholder=20
if a specified catastrophe occurs.  (Now you know why I suffer=20
eyestrain:  from watching The Weather Channel.)

     Currently, Berkshire is second in the U.S. property-casualty=20
industry in net worth (the leader being State Farm, which neither=20
buys nor sells reinsurance).  Therefore, we have the capacity to=20
assume risk on a scale that interests virtually no other company.=20
We have the appetite as well:  As Berkshire's net worth and=20
earnings grow, our willingness to write business increases also.=20
But let me add that means good business.  The saying, "a fool=20
and his money are soon invited everywhere," applies in spades in=20
reinsurance, and we actually reject more than 98% of the business=20
we are offered.  Our ability to choose between good and bad=20
proposals reflects a management strength that matches our=20
financial strength:  Ajit Jain, who runs our reinsurance=20
operation, is simply the best in this business.  In combination,=20
these strengths guarantee that we will stay a major factor in the=20
super-cat business so long as prices are appropriate.

     What constitutes an appropriate price, of course, is=20
difficult to determine.  Catastrophe insurers can't simply=20
extrapolate past experience.  If there is truly "global warming,"=20
for example, the odds would shift, since tiny changes in=20
atmospheric conditions can produce momentous changes in weather=20
patterns.  Furthermore, in recent years there has been a=20
mushrooming of population and insured values in U.S. coastal=20
areas that are particularly vulnerable to hurricanes, the number=20
one creator of super-cats.  A hurricane that caused <i>x</i> dollars of=20
damage 20 years ago could easily cost 10<i>x</i> now.

     Occasionally, also, the unthinkable happens.  Who would have=20
guessed, for example, that a major earthquake could occur in=20
Charleston, S.C.? (It struck in 1886, registered an estimated 6.6=20
on the Richter scale, and caused 60 deaths.)  And who could have=20
imagined that our country's most serious quake would occur at New=20
Madrid, Missouri, which suffered an estimated 8.7 shocker in=20
1812.  By comparison, the 1989 San Francisco quake was a 7.1 -=20
and remember that each one-point Richter increase represents a=20
ten-fold increase in strength.  Someday, a U.S. earthquake=20
occurring far from California will cause enormous losses for=20
insurers.

     When viewing our quarterly figures, you should understand=20
that our accounting for super-cat premiums differs from our=20
accounting for other insurance premiums.  Rather than recording=20
our super-cat premiums on a pro-rata basis over the life of a=20
given policy, we defer recognition of revenue until a loss occurs=20
or until the policy expires.  We take this conservative approach=20
because the likelihood of super-cats causing us losses is=20
particularly great toward the end of the year.  It is then that=20
weather tends to kick up:  Of the ten largest insured losses in=20
U.S. history, nine occurred in the last half of the year.  In=20
addition, policies that are not triggered by a first event are=20
unlikely, by their very terms, to cause us losses until late in=20
the year.

     The bottom-line effect of our accounting procedure for=20
super-cats is this:  Large losses may be reported in any quarter=20
of the year, but significant profits will only be reported in the=20
fourth quarter.

