                    * * * * * * * * * * * *

     Our equity-investing strategy remains little changed from what=20
it was fifteen years ago, when we said in the 1977 annual report: =20
"We select our marketable equity securities in much the way we=20
would evaluate a business for acquisition in its entirety.  We want=20
the business to be one (a) that we can understand; (b) with=20
favorable long-term prospects; (c) operated by honest and competent=20
people; and (d) available at a very attractive price."  We have=20
seen cause to make only one change in this creed: Because of both=20
market conditions and our size, we now substitute "an attractive=20
price" for "a very attractive price."

     But how, you will ask, does one decide what's "attractive"? =20
In answering this question, most analysts feel they must choose=20
between two approaches customarily thought to be in opposition: =20
"value" and "growth."  Indeed, many investment professionals see=20
any mixing of the two terms as a form of intellectual cross-
dressing.

     We view that as fuzzy thinking (in which, it must be=20
confessed, I myself engaged some years ago).  In our opinion, the=20
two approaches are joined at the hip:  Growth is <i>always</i> a component=
=20
in the calculation of value, constituting a variable whose=20
importance can range from negligible to enormous and whose impact=20
can be negative as well as positive.

     In addition, we think the very term "value investing" is=20
redundant.  What is "investing" if it is not the act of seeking=20
value at least sufficient to justify the amount paid?  Consciously=20
paying more for a stock than its calculated value - in the hope=20
that it can soon be sold for a still-higher price - should be=20
labeled speculation (which is neither illegal, immoral nor - in our=20
view - financially fattening).

     Whether appropriate or not, the term "value investing" is=20
widely used.  Typically, it connotes the purchase of stocks having=20
attributes such as a low ratio of price to book value, a low price-
earnings ratio, or a high dividend yield.  Unfortunately, such=20
characteristics, even if they appear in combination, are far from=20
determinative as to whether an investor is indeed buying something=20
for what it is worth and is therefore truly operating on the=20
principle of obtaining value in his investments.  Correspondingly,=20
opposite characteristics - a high ratio of price to book value, a=20
high price-earnings ratio, and a low dividend yield - are in no way=20
inconsistent with a "value" purchase.

     Similarly, business growth, per se, tells us little about=20
value.  It's true that growth often has a positive impact on value,=20
sometimes one of spectacular proportions.  But such an effect is=20
far from certain.  For example, investors have regularly poured=20
money into the domestic airline business to finance profitless (or=20
worse) growth.  For these investors, it would have been far better=20
if Orville had failed to get off the ground at Kitty Hawk: The more=20
the industry has grown, the worse the disaster for owners.

     Growth benefits investors only when the business in point can=20
invest at incremental returns that are enticing - in other words,=20
only when each dollar used to finance the growth creates over a=20
dollar of long-term market value.  In the case of a low-return=20
business requiring incremental funds, growth hurts the investor.

     In <u>The Theory of Investment Value</u>, written over 50 years ago,=
=20
John Burr Williams set forth the equation for value, which we=20
condense here:  <i>The value of any stock, bond or business today is=20
determined by the cash inflows and outflows - discounted at an=20
appropriate interest rate - that can be expected to occur during=20
the remaining life of the asset.</i>  Note that the formula is the same=20
for stocks as for bonds.  Even so, there is an important, and=20
difficult to deal with, difference between the two:  A bond has a=20
coupon and maturity date that define future cash flows; but in the=20
case of equities, the investment analyst must himself estimate the=20
future "coupons."  Furthermore, the quality of management affects=20
the bond coupon only rarely - chiefly when management is so inept=20
or dishonest that payment of interest is suspended.  In contrast,=20
the ability of management can dramatically affect the equity=20
"coupons."

     The investment shown by the discounted-flows-of-cash=20
calculation to be the cheapest is the one that the investor should=20
purchase - irrespective of whether the business grows or doesn't,=20
displays volatility or smoothness in its earnings, or carries a=20
high price or low in relation to its current earnings and book=20
value.  Moreover, though the value equation has usually shown=20
equities to be cheaper than bonds, that result is not inevitable: =20
When bonds are calculated to be the more attractive investment,=20
they should be bought.

     Leaving the question of price aside, the best business to own=20
is one that over an extended period can employ large amounts of=20
incremental capital at very high rates of return.  The worst=20
business to own is one that must, or <i>will</i>, do the opposite - that=20
is, consistently employ ever-greater amounts of capital at very low=20
rates of return.  Unfortunately, the first type of business is very=20
hard to find:  Most high-return businesses need relatively little=20
capital.  Shareholders of such a business usually will benefit if=20
it pays out most of its earnings in dividends or makes significant=20
stock repurchases.

     Though the mathematical calculations required to evaluate=20
equities are not difficult, an analyst - even one who is=20
experienced and intelligent - can easily go wrong in estimating=20
future "coupons."  At Berkshire, we attempt to deal with this=20
problem in two ways.  First, we try to stick to businesses we=20
believe we understand.  That means they must be relatively simple=20
and stable in character.  If a business is complex or subject to=20
constant change, we're not smart enough to predict future cash=20
flows.  Incidentally, that shortcoming doesn't bother us.  What=20
counts for most people in investing is not how much they know, but=20
rather how realistically they define what they don't know.  An=20
investor needs to do very few things right as long as he or she=20
avoids big mistakes.

     Second, and equally important, we insist on a margin of safety=20
in our purchase price.  If we calculate the value of a common stock=20
to be only slightly higher than its price, we're not interested in=20
buying.  We believe this margin-of-safety principle, so strongly=20
emphasized by Ben Graham, to be the cornerstone of investment=20
success.

