<b>Two New Accounting Rules and a Plea for One More</b>

     A new accounting rule having to do with deferred taxes becomes=20
effective in 1993.  It undoes a dichotomy in our books that I have=20
described in previous annual reports and that relates to the=20
accrued taxes carried against the unrealized appreciation in our=20
investment portfolio.  At yearend 1992, that appreciation amounted=20
to $7.6 billion.  Against $6.4 billion of that, we carried taxes at=20
the current 34% rate.  Against the remainder of $1.2 billion, we=20
carried an accrual of 28%, the tax rate in effect when that portion=20
of the appreciation occurred.  The new accounting rule says we must=20
henceforth accrue all deferred tax at the current rate, which to us=20
seems sensible.

     The new marching orders mean that in the first quarter of 1993=20
we will apply a 34% rate to all of our unrealized appreciation,=20
thereby increasing the tax liability and reducing net worth by $70=20
million.  The new rule also will cause us to make other minor=20
changes in our calculation of deferred taxes.

     Future changes in tax rates will be reflected immediately in=20
the liability for deferred taxes and, correspondingly, in net=20
worth.  The impact could well be substantial.  Nevertheless, what=20
is important in the end is the tax rate at the time we sell=20
securities, when unrealized appreciation becomes realized.

     Another major accounting change, whose implementation is=20
required by January 1, 1993, mandates that businesses recognize=20
their present-value liability for post-retirement health benefits.=20
Though GAAP has previously required recognition of pensions to be=20
paid in the future, it has illogically ignored the costs that=20
companies will then have to bear for health benefits.  The new rule=20
will force many companies to record a huge balance-sheet liability=20
(and a consequent reduction in net worth) and also henceforth to=20
recognize substantially higher costs when they are calculating=20
annual profits.

     In making acquisitions, Charlie and I have tended to avoid=20
companies with significant post-retirement liabilities.  As a=20
result, Berkshire's present liability and future costs for post-
retirement health benefits - though we now have 22,000 employees -=20
are inconsequential.  I need to admit, though, that we had a near=20
miss:  In 1982 I made a huge mistake in committing to buy a company=20
burdened by extraordinary post-retirement health obligations. =20
Luckily, though, the transaction fell through for reasons beyond=20
our control.  Reporting on this episode in the 1982 annual report,=20
I said:  "If we were to introduce graphics to this report,=20
illustrating favorable business developments of the past year, two=20
blank pages depicting this blown deal would be the appropriate=20
centerfold."  Even so, I wasn't expecting things to get as bad as=20
they did.  Another buyer appeared, the business soon went bankrupt=20
and was shut down, and thousands of workers found those bountiful=20
health-care promises to be largely worthless.

     In recent decades, no CEO would have dreamed of going to his=20
board with the proposition that his company become an insurer of=20
uncapped post-retirement health benefits that other corporations=20
chose to install.  A CEO didn't need to be a medical expert to know=20
that lengthening life expectancies and soaring health costs would=20
guarantee an insurer a financial battering from such a business. =20
Nevertheless, many a manager blithely committed his own company to=20
a self-insurance plan embodying precisely the same promises - and=20
thereby doomed his shareholders to suffer the inevitable=20
consequences.  In health-care, open-ended promises have created=20
open-ended liabilities that in a few cases loom so large as to=20
threaten the global competitiveness of major American industries.

     I believe part of the reason for this reckless behavior was=20
that accounting rules did not, for so long, require the booking of=20
post-retirement health costs as they were incurred.  Instead, the=20
rules allowed cash-basis accounting, which vastly understated the=20
liabilities that were building up.  In effect, the attitude of both=20
managements and their accountants toward these liabilities was=20
"out-of-sight, out-of-mind."  Ironically, some of these same=20
managers would be quick to criticize Congress for employing "cash-
basis" thinking in respect to Social Security promises or other=20
programs creating future liabilities of size.

     Managers thinking about accounting issues should never forget=20
one of Abraham Lincoln's favorite riddles:  "How many legs does a=20
dog have if you call his tail a leg?"  The answer:  "Four, because=20
calling a tail a leg does not make it a leg."  It behooves managers=20
to remember that Abe's right even if an auditor is willing to=20
certify that the tail is a leg.

                   * * * * * * * * * * * *

     The most egregious case of let's-not-face-up-to-reality=20
behavior by executives and accountants has occurred in the world of=20
stock options.  In Berkshire's 1985 annual report, I laid out my=20
opinions about the use and misuse of options.  But even when=20
options are structured properly, they are accounted for in ways=20
that make no sense.  The lack of logic is not accidental:  For=20
decades, much of the business world has waged war against=20
accounting rulemakers, trying to keep the costs of stock options=20
from being reflected in the profits of the corporations that issue=20
them.

     Typically, executives have argued that options are hard to=20
value and that therefore their costs should be ignored.  At other=20
times managers have said that assigning a cost to options would=20
injure small start-up businesses.  Sometimes they have even=20
solemnly declared that "out-of-the-money" options (those with an=20
exercise price equal to or above the current market price) have no=20
value when they are issued.

     Oddly, the Council of Institutional Investors has chimed in=20
with a variation on that theme, opining that options should not be=20
viewed as a cost because they "aren't dollars out of a company's=20
coffers."  I see this line of reasoning as offering exciting=20
possibilities to American corporations for instantly improving=20
their reported profits.  For example, they could eliminate the cost=20
of insurance by paying for it with options.  So if you're a CEO and=20
subscribe to this "no cash-no cost" theory of accounting, I'll make=20
you an offer you can't refuse:  Give us a call at Berkshire and we=20
will happily sell you insurance in exchange for a bundle of long-
term options on your company's stock.

     Shareholders should understand that companies incur costs when=20
they deliver something of value to another party and not just when=20
cash changes hands.  Moreover, it is both silly and cynical to say=20
that an important item of cost should not be recognized simply=20
because it can't be quantified with pinpoint precision.  Right now,=20
accounting abounds with imprecision.  After all, no manager or=20
auditor knows how long a 747 is going to last, which means he also=20
does not know what the yearly depreciation charge for the plane=20
should be.  No one knows with any certainty what a bank's annual=20
loan loss charge ought to be.  And the estimates of losses that=20
property-casualty companies make are notoriously inaccurate.

     Does this mean that these important items of cost should be=20
ignored simply because they can't be quantified with absolute=20
accuracy?  Of course not.  Rather, these costs should be estimated=20
by honest and experienced people and then recorded.  When you get=20
right down to it, what other item of major but hard-to-precisely-
calculate cost - other, that is, than stock options - does the=20
accounting profession say should be ignored in the calculation of=20
earnings?

     Moreover, options are just not that difficult to value. =20
Admittedly, the difficulty is increased by the fact that the=20
options given to executives are restricted in various ways.  These=20
restrictions affect value.  They do not, however, eliminate it.  In=20
fact, since I'm in the mood for offers, I'll make one to any=20
executive who is granted a restricted option, even though it may be=20
out of the money:  On the day of issue, Berkshire will pay him or=20
her a substantial sum for the right to any future gain he or she=20
realizes on the option.  So if you find a CEO who says his newly-
issued options have little or no value, tell him to try us out.  In=20
truth, we have far more confidence in our ability to determine an=20
appropriate price to pay for an option than we have in our ability=20
to determine the proper depreciation rate for our corporate jet.

     It seems to me that the realities of stock options can be=20
summarized quite simply:  If options aren't a form of compensation,=20
what are they?  If compensation isn't an expense, what is it?  And,=20
if expenses shouldn't go into the calculation of earnings, where in=20
the world should they go?

     The accounting profession and the SEC should be shamed by the=20
fact that they have long let themselves be muscled by business=20
executives on the option-accounting issue.  Additionally, the=20
lobbying that executives engage in may have an unfortunate by-
product:  In my opinion, the business elite risks losing its=20
credibility on issues of significance to society - about which it=20
may have much of value to say - when it advocates the incredible on=20
issues of significance to itself.

<b>Miscellaneous</b>

     We have two pieces of regrettable news this year.  First,=20
Gladys Kaiser, my friend and assistant for twenty-five years, will=20
give up the latter post after the 1993 annual meeting, though she=20
will certainly remain my friend forever.  Gladys and I have been a=20
team, and though I knew her retirement was coming, it is still a=20
jolt.

     Secondly, in September, Verne McKenzie relinquished his role=20
as Chief Financial Officer after a 30-year association with me that=20
began when he was the outside auditor of Buffett Partnership, Ltd.=20
Verne is staying on as a consultant, and though that job=20
description is often a euphemism, in this case it has real meaning.=20
I expect Verne to continue to fill an important role at Berkshire=20
but to do so at his own pace.  Marc Hamburg, Verne's understudy for=20
five years, has succeeded him as Chief Financial Officer.

     I recall that one woman, upon being asked to describe the=20
perfect spouse, specified an archeologist: "The older I get," she=20
said, "the more he'll be interested in me."  She would have liked=20
my tastes:  I treasure those extraordinary Berkshire managers who=20
are working well past normal retirement age and who concomitantly=20
are achieving results much superior to those of their younger=20
competitors.  While I understand and empathize with the decision of=20
Verne and Gladys to retire when the calendar says it's time, theirs=20
is not a step I wish to encourage.  It's hard to teach a new dog=20
old tricks.

                  * * * * * * * * * * * *=20

     I am a moderate in my views about retirement compared to Rose=20
Blumkin, better known as Mrs. B.  At 99, she continues to work=20
seven days a week.  And about her, I have some particularly good=20
news.

     You will remember that after her family sold 80% of Nebraska=20
Furniture Mart (NFM) to Berkshire in 1983, Mrs. B continued to be=20
Chairman and run the carpet operation.  In 1989, however, she left=20
because of a managerial disagreement and opened up her own=20
operation next door in a large building that she had owned for=20
several years.  In her new business, she ran the carpet section but=20
leased out other home-furnishings departments.

     At the end of last year, Mrs. B decided to sell her building=20
and land to NFM.  She'll continue, however, to run her carpet=20
business at its current location (no sense slowing down just when=20
you're hitting full stride).  NFM will set up shop alongside her,=20
in that same building, thereby making a major addition to its=20
furniture business.

     I am delighted that Mrs. B has again linked up with us.  Her=20
business story has no parallel and I have always been a fan of=20
hers, whether she was a partner or a competitor.  But believe me,=20
partner is better.

     This time around, Mrs. B graciously offered to sign a non-
compete agreement - and I, having been incautious on this point=20
when she was 89, snapped at the deal.  Mrs. B belongs in the=20
Guinness Book of World Records on many counts.  Signing a non-
compete at 99 merely adds one more.

                   * * * * * * * * * * * *

     Ralph Schey, CEO of Scott Fetzer and a manager who I hope is=20
with us at 99 also, hit a grand slam last year when that company=20
earned a record $110 million pre-tax.  What's even more impressive=20
is that Scott Fetzer achieved such earnings while employing only=20
$116 million of equity capital.  This extraordinary result is not=20
the product of leverage:  The company uses only minor amounts of=20
borrowed money (except for the debt it employs - appropriately - in=20
its finance subsidiary).

     Scott Fetzer now operates with a significantly smaller=20
investment in both inventory and fixed assets than it had when we=20
bought it in 1986.  This means the company has been able to=20
distribute more than 100% of its earnings to Berkshire during our=20
seven years of ownership while concurrently increasing its earnings=20
stream - which was excellent to begin with - by a lot.  Ralph just=20
keeps on outdoing himself, and Berkshire shareholders owe him a=20
great deal.

                   * * * * * * * * * * * *

     Those readers with particularly sharp eyes will note that our=20
corporate expense fell from $5.6 million in 1991 to $4.2 million in=20
1992.  Perhaps you will think that I have sold our corporate jet,=20
The Indefensible.  Forget it!  I find the thought of retiring the=20
plane even more revolting than the thought of retiring the=20
Chairman.  (In this matter I've demonstrated uncharacteristic=20
flexibility:  For years I argued passionately against corporate=20
jets.  But finally my dogma was run over by my karma.)

     Our reduction in corporate overhead actually came about=20
because those expenses were especially high in 1991, when we=20
incurred a one-time environmental charge relating to alleged pre-
1970 actions of our textile operation.  Now that things are back to=20
normal, our after-tax overhead costs are under 1% of our reported=20
operating earnings and less than 1/2 of 1% of our look-through=20
earnings.  We have no legal, personnel, public relations, investor=20
relations, or strategic planning departments.  In turn this means=20
we don't need support personnel such as guards, drivers,=20
messengers, etc.  Finally, except for Verne, we employ no=20
consultants.  Professor Parkinson would like our operation - though=20
Charlie, I must say, still finds it outrageously fat.

     At some companies, corporate expense runs 10% or more of=20
operating earnings.  The tithing that operations thus makes to=20
headquarters not only hurts earnings, but more importantly slashes=20
capital values.  If the business that spends 10% on headquarters'=20
costs achieves earnings at its operating levels identical to those=20
achieved by the business that incurs costs of only 1%, shareholders=20
of the first enterprise suffer a 9% loss in the value of their=20
holdings simply because of corporate overhead.  Charlie and I have=20
observed no correlation between high corporate costs and good=20
corporate performance.  In fact, we see the simpler, low-cost=20
operation as more likely to operate effectively than its=20
bureaucratic brethren.  We're admirers of the Wal-Mart, Nucor,=20
Dover, GEICO, Golden West Financial and Price Co. models.

                   * * * * * * * * * * * *

     Late last year Berkshire's stock price crossed $10,000. =20
Several shareholders have mentioned to me that the high price=20
causes them problems:  They like to give shares away each year and=20
find themselves impeded by the tax rule that draws a distinction=20
between annual gifts of $10,000 or under to a single individual and=20
those above $10,000.  That is, those gifts no greater than $10,000=20
are completely tax-free; those above $10,000 require the donor to=20
use up a portion of his or her lifetime exemption from gift and=20
estate taxes, or, if that exemption has been exhausted, to pay gift=20
taxes.

     I can suggest three ways to address this problem.  The first=20
would be useful to a married shareholder, who can give up to=20
$20,000 annually to a single recipient, as long as the donor files=20
a gift tax return containing his or her spouse's written consent to=20
gifts made during the year.

     Secondly, a shareholder, married or not, can make a bargain=20
sale.  Imagine, for example, that Berkshire is selling for $12,000=20
and that one wishes to make only a $10,000 gift.  In that case,=20
sell the stock to the giftee for $2,000.  (Caution:  You will be=20
taxed on the amount, if any, by which the sales price to your=20
giftee exceeds your tax basis.)

     Finally, you can establish a partnership with people to whom=20
you are making gifts, fund it with Berkshire shares, and simply=20
give percentage interests in the partnership away each year.  These=20
interests can be for any value that you select.  If the value is=20
$10,000 or less, the gift will be tax-free.

     We issue the customary warning:  Consult with your own tax=20
advisor before taking action on any of the more esoteric methods of=20
gift-making.

     We hold to the view about stock splits that we set forth in=20
the 1983 Annual Report.  Overall, we believe our owner-related=20
policies - including the no-split policy - have helped us assemble=20
a body of shareholders that is the best associated with any widely-
held American corporation.  Our shareholders think and behave like=20
rational long-term owners and view the business much as Charlie and=20
I do.  Consequently, our stock consistently trades in a price range=20
that is sensibly related to intrinsic value.

     Additionally, we believe that our shares turn over far less=20
actively than do the shares of any other widely-held company.  The=20
frictional costs of trading - which act as a major "tax" on the=20
owners of many companies - are virtually non-existent at Berkshire.=20
(The market-making skills of Jim Maguire, our New York Stock=20
Exchange specialist, definitely help to keep these costs low.) =20
Obviously a split would not change this situation dramatically. =20
Nonetheless, there is no way that our shareholder group would be=20
upgraded by the new shareholders enticed by a split.  Instead we=20
believe that modest degradation would occur.

                   * * * * * * * * * * * *

     As I mentioned earlier, on December 16th we called our zero-
coupon, convertible debentures for payment on January 4, 1993. =20
These obligations bore interest at 5 1/2%, a low cost for funds=20
when they were issued in 1989, but an unattractive rate for us at=20
the time of call.

     The debentures could have been redeemed at the option of the=20
holder in September 1994, and 5 1/2% money available for no longer=20
than that is not now of interest to us.  Furthermore, Berkshire=20
shareholders are disadvantaged by having a conversion option=20
outstanding.  At the time we issued the debentures, this=20
disadvantage was offset by the attractive interest rate they=20
carried; by late 1992, it was not.

     In general, we continue to have an aversion to debt,=20
particularly the short-term kind.  But we are willing to incur=20
modest amounts of debt when it is both properly structured and of=20
significant benefit to shareholders.


